##### C++

```cpp
// mark "seen" by negating the pointed-at cell; O(1) extra space
vector<int> fad448Sign(vector<int> nums) {
    int n = (int)nums.size();
    for (int x : nums) {
        int idx = abs(x) - 1;
        if (nums[idx] > 0) nums[idx] = -nums[idx];
    }
    vector<int> ans;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)
        if (nums[i] > 0) ans.push_back(i + 1);
    return ans;
}
```
:::

**Reading it line by line.** `abs(x)` is essential in the second pass because cells have already been flipped negative — we read the *original* value by magnitude. The Python version copies into `a` to keep the stress test's input reusable; the C++ version mutates its by-value parameter. Notice the array became a histogram of presence ($0$ or $\ge 1$ per bucket), stored entirely in its own sign bits — the purest example of "the value is the address."

:::

::: {.p-complexity}
**Set difference** is $O(n)$ time, $O(n)$ space. **Sign marking** is $O(n)$ time, $O(1)$ extra space (the answer list excluded). Both are linear; the second spends no memory because the value range $[1, n]$ lets the array index itself.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- All equal `[1,1,...,1]`: slot 0 marked, every other slot stays positive → the whole tail $[2..n]$ is missing.
- Full permutation (each once): nothing missing → `[]`.
- `n = 1`: the single value is `1`, so `[]`; a value outside `[1, n]` cannot occur by the constraint.
:::

::: {.practice}
- [LC 442 · Find All Duplicates in an Array](https://leetcode.com/problems/find-all-duplicates-in-an-array/){difficulty="Medium"} — the same sign marking, reporting the slots hit twice instead of never.
- [LC 268 · Missing Number](https://leetcode.com/problems/missing-number/){difficulty="Easy"} — values $0..n$ with one gap: sign marking, cyclic placement, or a sum.
:::


[**From "which slots stayed positive" to "send every value home".** LC 448 marks seen values and reads the untouched slots. LC 41 must cope with values outside $[1, n]$ (negatives, zero, huge numbers), so it guards the index and places each in-range value at its home instead.]{.flow-bridge}

### Problem: First Missing Positive {#sec-lts-p2-b}

::: {.p-statement}

Given an unsorted integer array `nums` ($1 \le n \le 10^5$, $-2^{31} \le \mathit{nums}[i] \le 2^{31}-1$ — negatives and huge values allowed), return the smallest positive integer not present. The demand is explicit: $O(n)$ **time** and $O(1)$ **auxiliary space**.

Source: [LC 41 · First Missing Positive](https://leetcode.com/problems/first-missing-positive/){.solved difficulty="Hard"}

Example — `nums = [3, 4, -1, 1]`. Output `2` (1 is present, 2 is not).
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
The answer is always in $[1, n+1]$: among $n$ cells at most $n$ distinct positives $1..n$ fit, so if all of them appear the gap is $n+1$. That bounds the search to the array's own size — and therefore lets us *store the answer set inside the array*. Cyclic sort does it: value $v \in [1, n]$ belongs at index $v-1$, so swap each into place; the first index holding the wrong value names the missing number. Unlike @sec-lts-p2-a, values here can fall outside $[1, n]$, so we only place the ones in range.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Set scan.** Put everything in a hash set, then test $1, 2, 3, \dots$ until one is absent. $O(n)$ time but $O(n)$ space — breaks the space rule; the oracle.
2. **Cyclic placement.** Walk indices; while `nums[i]` is in $[1, n]$ and its home slot doesn't already hold it, swap it home. Afterwards the first $i$ with `nums[i] != i+1` gives the answer, else $n+1$. $O(n)$ time, $O(1)$ space.

**Invariant.** Each successful swap puts one value permanently at its correct index (it is never touched again, since it is already home), so the loop performs at most $n$ swaps in total; when it ends, `nums[i] == i+1` exactly for the present values $1..n$.
:::

The buttons are in order: pay $O(n)$ memory for a set, then fold the set into the array with swaps.

::: {.panel-tabset .solution-forms}

#### Set scan

::: {.form-next}
Hash the values, then march an integer upward from $1$ and stop at the first not in the set. Correct in $O(n)$ time, but $O(n)$ space the problem forbids.

**Worked example.** seen $= \{-1, 1, 3, 4\}$; test $1$ (yes), $2$ (no) → answer $2$.
:::

