// bucket sort by frequency, linear after the histogram
string scf451Bucket(string s) {
    unordered_map<char, int> c;
    for (char x : s) ++c[x];
    int n = (int)s.size();
    vector<vector<char>> buckets(n + 1);
    for (auto& p : c) buckets[p.second].push_back(p.first);
    string res;
    for (int f = n; f >= 1; --f) {
        sort(buckets[f].begin(), buckets[f].end());
        for (char ch : buckets[f]) for (int k = 0; k < f; ++k) res.push_back(ch);
    }
    return res;
}
```
:::

**Reading it line by line.** A frequency of $f$ can only live in a bucket index $\le n$, which is why `n + 1` buckets suffice — the count is a *valid array index*, the entire licence for bucket sort. The `Counter(...) == Counter(s)` assertion confirms the output is a genuine rearrangement (same multiset), and the exact-equality assertion ties the two forms down to one deterministic answer so the stress test is meaningful.

:::

::: {.p-complexity}
**Count + sort** is $O(n + A \log A)$ time, $O(n)$ space. **Bucket sort** is $O(n + A)$ time, $O(n)$ space. Since $A \le 62$ (letters + digits) both are linear here; bucket sort's value is that it never *compares* two characters.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- Ties (`cccaaa`): both `a` and `c` count 3, so they share a bucket and may come in any order — accepted.
- Every character distinct: all land in bucket 1; any permutation is valid.
- Case + digits are distinct symbols (`Aabb`: `b` 2×, then `A`, `a` 1× each).
:::

::: {.practice}
- [LC 1636 · Sort Array by Increasing Frequency](https://leetcode.com/problems/sort-array-by-increasing-frequency/){difficulty="Easy"} — frequency first, value second: buckets by count, each bucket in descending value order.
- [LC 692 · Top K Frequent Words](https://leetcode.com/problems/top-k-frequent-words/){difficulty="Medium"} — frequency buckets whose ties must be broken alphabetically.
:::


[**From emitting every bucket to stopping at $k$.** LC 451 walks all frequency buckets to rebuild the whole string. LC 347 uses the same buckets but stops once $k$ values are collected: the same count-as-index structure with an early exit, $O(n)$ where `most_common` is $O(n + A\log A)$.]{.flow-bridge}

### Problem: Top K Frequent Elements {#sec-lts-p3-b}

::: {.p-statement}

Given `nums` ($1 \le n \le 10^5$, $-10^4 \le \mathit{nums}[i] \le 10^4$) and $k$ ($1 \le k \le$ #distinct), return the $k$ most frequent elements in any order; the answer set is guaranteed unique. The follow-up demands better than $O(n \log n)$.

Source: [LC 347 · Top K Frequent Elements](https://leetcode.com/problems/top-k-frequent-elements/){.solved difficulty="Medium"}

Example — `nums = [1,1,1,2,2,3], k = 2`. Output `[1, 2]` (counts 3 and 2).
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
Sorting values by frequency is $O(n \log n)$. But frequencies are themselves small integers in $[1, n]$, so invert the buckets: index an array *by count* and store the values having that count. Walking the buckets from $n$ down collects the most common values first — stop after $k$. No comparisons; total $O(n)$. This is @sec-lts-p3-a's idea, cut short.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Count, sort by count.** `Counter.most_common(k)` sorts the distinct values by frequency and slices. $O(n + A \log A)$ — the oracle (and, for $A$ distinct values, only mildly superlinear).
2. **Frequency buckets.** Histogram, then `buckets[count].append(value)`; scan $f = n \downarrow 1$ collecting until $k$ values are gathered. $O(n)$ time, $O(n)$ space.

**Invariant.** A value is stored in exactly the bucket equal to its frequency, so a downward scan visits values in non-increasing frequency; the first $k$ collected are the $k$ most frequent, and the uniqueness guarantee means the cut at $k$ never lands mid-tie.
:::

The buttons are in order: lean on `most_common`, then beat it with the count-as-index trick.

::: {.panel-tabset .solution-forms}

#### Count and sort

::: {.form-next}
Histogram the values, take the top $k$ of `most_common`. $O(n + A \log A)$, $O(A)$ space; correct and the baseline for the stress test.

**Worked example.** `1:3, 2:2, 3:1`; most_common(2) → `[1, 2]`.
:::

