```cpp
// bucket values by frequency, harvest the top k in O(n)
vector<int> tkf347Bucket(vector<int> nums, int k) {
    unordered_map<int, int> c;
    for (int x : nums) ++c[x];
    int n = (int)nums.size();
    vector<vector<int>> buckets(n + 1);
    for (auto& p : c) buckets[p.second].push_back(p.first);
    vector<int> res;
    for (int f = n; f >= 1 && (int)res.size() < k; --f)
        for (int x : buckets[f]) { res.push_back(x); if ((int)res.size() == k) break; }
    return res;
}
```
:::

**Reading it line by line.** The `if len(res) == k: return` short-circuit is the difference between @sec-lts-p3-a (emit the *whole* sorted string) and here (stop at $k$): same bucket structure, earlier exit. The stress test compares as *sets* (`sorted(...)`) because the problem accepts any order, and it skips random inputs where the $k$-th and $(k+1)$-th counts tie: the problem promises those never occur, and on them two correct programs may legitimately return different sets.

:::

::: {.p-complexity}
**Count + sort** is $O(n + A \log A)$ time. **Frequency buckets** is $O(n)$ time and $O(n)$ space. A third route — a size-$k$ min-heap of (count, value) — is $O(n + A \log k)$; see @sec-salgo for the heap view of "keep the best $k$."
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- $k$ equals the number of distinct values: the whole value set, in frequency order.
- A value counted once lives in `bucket[1]`; it is only taken if the window reaches count 1.
- Single element `[1], k=1` → `[1]`.
:::

::: {.practice}
- [LC 1481 · Least Number of Unique Integers after K Removals](https://leetcode.com/problems/least-number-of-unique-integers-after-k-removals/){difficulty="Medium"} — the same frequency buckets, scanned from the *smallest* count up.
:::


## Pattern 4: Positional and histogram equality {#sec-lts-p4}

**From sorting to answering.** Sometimes no order is needed in the output at all. Either the data already carries each item's destination and the only job is to write it there (@sec-lts-p4-a), or the question is just whether two multisets are equal (@sec-lts-p4-b). The first is the placement phase of counting sort with the positions handed over; the second is its counting phase used as an equality test.

**Recognise it.** "Each item carries its own position" → direct placement. "Are these the same multiset / anagrams?" → compare counts, never sort.

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
Use the key as an address to write, or as an address to tally.

```text
place:  out[key - 1] = payload                     # the embedded index IS the slot (A1, A2)
equal:  one counter, += for A, -= for B; equal iff every slot is 0   (A6)
```

**Invariant.** If the keys form a permutation of $1..n$, direct placement fills every slot exactly once; a shared histogram nets to zero iff the two multisets are identical.
:::

**Complexity.** Both $O(n)$ time; placement uses $O(n)$ space for the output, the equality test $O(1)$ (fixed alphabet) or $O(A)$ for a general alphabet. Sorting both is the $O(n\log n)$ fallback.

### Problem: Sorting the Sentence {#sec-lts-p4-a}

::: {.p-statement}

A sentence is words separated by single spaces, no leading/trailing spaces. It is *shuffled* by appending each word's 1-indexed position to its end and rearranging. Given `s` ($2 \le |s| \le 200$; between 1 and 9 words; letters, spaces, and digits `1`–`9`), reconstruct the original sentence.

Source: [LC 1859 · Sorting the Sentence](https://leetcode.com/problems/sorting-the-sentence/){.solved difficulty="Easy"}

Example — `s = "is2 sentence4 This1 a3"`. Output `"This is a sentence"` (each word carries its slot; strip the digits).
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
Every word already names its own destination: the trailing digit *is* the index. That is the counting-sort situation in its purest form — the key is a small integer in a known range $[1, w]$, so instead of comparing words, write each straight into slot `d-1`. This is @sec-lts-a1's "value as address" with the address handed to us for free.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Sort by the trailing character.** Split on spaces, `sorted(ws, key=lambda w: w[-1])`, then strip the last character of each and join. $O(w \log w)$ comparisons on single characters — the oracle.
2. **Direct placement.** Allocate `out = [None] * w`; for each word write `out[int(w[-1]) - 1] = w[:-1]`; join. $O(n)$, no comparisons at all.

**Invariant.** Because the digits are exactly the permutation $1..w$, after placement every slot holds precisely the word that belonged there, so reading slots left-to-right is the original sentence.
:::

The buttons are in order: lean on a comparison sort of the digits, then drop the comparisons and index directly.

::: {.panel-tabset .solution-forms}

#### Sort by embedded index

::: {.form-next}
Split into words, order them by their final character (the position digit), strip the digits, join. $O(w \log w)$ time, $O(n)$ space — correct and the baseline.

**Worked example.** `["is2","sentence4","This1","a3"]` sorted by last char → `This1, is2, a3, sentence4` → `"This is a sentence"`.
:::

